INSERT INTO inform.politician_context (politician_id, topic_id, reasoning, sources)
SELECT '171dbfaa-58dd-45f4-8526-231d78530461', ct.id, $ctx$In a Feb. 19, 2026 press release on SB23, which extends Wisconsin's postpartum Medicaid coverage from 60 days to a full year, Hesselbein called it a policy that "will save lives, improve the health of moms and babies" and said she had championed the extension for years. This is a concrete, significant Medicaid expansion she personally pushed, aligning with chair 2's "expand Medicaid significantly" language. The release does not address Medicare eligibility age (the scale's other compound element), so this is a partial match, but it clearly rules out the cost-control (3) and privatization (4-5) chairs. Note for orchestrator: this may strengthen or supersede the pass-1 medicare/aid row currently under review. [ORCHESTRATOR REVIEW 2026-07-27: value corrected from 2 to 3. Chair 2 reads 'lower Medicare age to 55 AND expand Medicaid significantly' -- a compound chair whose Medicare-age element no state legislator can evidence, since Medicare is federal. The same bill (SB23) was independently scored chair 2 by two research passes and chair 3 by a third; the chair-3 reading is adopted because it is the only one that applies the compound-chair rule. Chair 3, 'improve current programs', is what a benefit extension does. The evidence cited above is unchanged and still supports the row -- only the chair mapping was corrected.]$ctx$,
